\title{cqtcpower: power and sample size for C-QTc analyses in parallel SAD studies}
\description{
The package answers two questions for a planned parallel-group single
ascending dose (SAD) study whose ECG data will be analysed with a
concentration-QTc (C-QTc) linear mixed-effects model:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{What is the power} to conclude "no QT effect" (upper bound of the
one-sided \eqn{1-\alpha} confidence interval of the predicted
\eqn{\Delta\Delta QTc} at the supratherapeutic concentration below
\eqn{U_0 = 10} ms) for a given number of subjects per cohort?
See \code{\link[=cqtc_power]{cqtc_power()}} and \code{\link[=cqtc_power_curve]{cqtc_power_curve()}}.
\item \strong{How many subjects per cohort} are needed to reach a target power?
See \code{\link[=cqtc_sample_size]{cqtc_sample_size()}}.
}

Analytical results use the closed-form covariance approximations for the
\code{RI}, \code{RI + Z}, \code{RS0} and \code{RS0 + Z} models. Simulation results
(\code{\link[=cqtc_simulate]{cqtc_simulate()}}) are available for all six models, including the
correlated random-slope models \code{RS} and \code{RS + Z}.

A typical workflow:
\enumerate{
\item describe the concentrations with \code{\link[=conc_pk_1cmt]{conc_pk_1cmt()}} or \code{\link[=conc_profile]{conc_profile()}};
\item describe the study with \code{\link[=cqtc_design]{cqtc_design()}};
\item set the true effect and variance components with \code{\link[=cqtc_params]{cqtc_params()}};
\item call \code{\link[=cqtc_power]{cqtc_power()}}, \code{\link[=cqtc_power_curve]{cqtc_power_curve()}}, \code{\link[=cqtc_sample_size]{cqtc_sample_size()}} or
\code{\link[=cqtc_simulate]{cqtc_simulate()}}.
}
}
